{
  "id": "have-got-a1",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "First words, first sentences",
  "title": "have got: what you have",
  "tagline": "Pets, family, hair and eyes: how to say what you've got — and what you haven't.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Speaking Part 1 — questions about you and your family",
    "Reading Part 5 — open cloze (grammar gaps)"
  ],
  "why": "<i>Have got</i> is how you talk about your family, your pets and your things, and how you describe people — <i>She's got long hair</i>. In Speaking Part 1 the examiner often asks about your family or your pets, so this is a verb you need in your very first answers.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "On a video call, Liam shows his screen. \"I<b>'ve got</b> a new puppy!\" he says. \"<b>Has</b> he <b>got</b> a name?\" asks Mateo. \"Yes — <b>he's got</b> a name, Rocky! <b>Have</b> you <b>got</b> any pets?\" \"No,\" says Mateo, \"I <b>haven't got</b> a pet — just a goldfish!\"",
    "ask": "The story uses have got, has got, haven't got and a question with Have. Can you find all four?"
  },
